  "slug": "regulatory-3p-6abe",
  "summary": "Single-cell, spatial, and multiomic profiling is resolving immune heterogeneity across autoimmune disease, cancer, and infection.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is single-cell transcriptomics applied to immune and tumor ecosystems to define cell states, lineage origins, and candidate signatures. Several reports nominate markers or prognostic models: a 69-gene NET-associated neutrophil signature during allogeneic CAR-γδT therapy, CD74 as a tissue biomarker for bullous pemphigoid, and a GZMK+/GZMB+ effector T-cell ratio that tracks health trajectories across a new cohort of 2,609 individuals. Others resolve ontogeny, such as CD24 and CD27 expression defining the differentiation of CD11c+ atypical memory B cells after malaria, or a PMN-MDSC-like ARG1-associated network in multiple sclerosis bone marrow. Spatial and multiomic layers extend this to niches associated with therapeutic resistance, including immune niches in clear cell renal cell carcinoma and MDK+ fibroblasts in breast cancer. The shared advance is resolving heterogeneity at cellular resolution rather than a single unifying mechanism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-15"
}
